# Local Difficulty Sensing Specification
# SSi Learning App
#
# Version: 1.0.0
# Last updated: 2026-06-13
# Grounded in: docs/methodology/metrics-architecture.md §4 (Sensing difficulty locally),
#              Principle 4 (difficulty is contextual)
#              docs/methodology/metrics-implementation-plan.md workstream B4
#              docs/methodology/adaptation-budget.md (the consumer: controller C)
#
# Core Insight: Difficulty is not a property of a phrase — it is a property of
# (THIS learner x THIS unit), NOW, and it moves as the map fills in. Sense it by
# running the curvature sensor (B1) LOCALLY: per LEGO, per word, and especially
# per BOUNDARY (the join between units). A developing struggle shows as positive
# acceleration against the unit's own noise BEFORE the level visibly moves.
#
# SENSING ONLY — no policy. Decides nothing about drill-vs-defer or return
# timing; that is C1, which combines this read with criticality (introduction
# order) + the spaced-repetition schedule. The sensor senses; the controller decides.
#
# Implementation Status (verified 2026-06-13):
# - assessLocalDifficulty(): LIVE  (packages/core/src/learning/localDifficulty.ts)
# - rankLocalDifficulty():   LIVE  (severity order; NOT a drill/defer decision)
# - Built on B1 curvature engine (computeCurvature + accelerationAlarm).
# - Live wiring to persisted per-(learner,unit) series (Layer 1 / A3 migration):
#   NOT DONE — caller's job once A3 lands. Pure compute is synthetic-verifiable now.

module: local_difficulty
  purpose: >
    Read whether a (learner, unit) is steady, a developing struggle, or easing,
    by running the curvature sensor over that unit's time-series. The leading
    indicator the adaptation controller spends as a consolidate/defer/drill budget
    and the dashboard renders as "dig deeper here".

  input_convention: >
    Higher value = more difficulty/struggle (e.g. normalised latency, |length-delta|).
    With that convention, positive acceleration = a struggle DEVELOPING; negative = easing.
    The sign is only interpretable if the caller honours the convention.

  data:
    UnitKind: enum [ lego, word, boundary ]
    UnitSeries:
      unitId: string
      unitKind: UnitKind?          # passed through, not interpreted
      values: number[]             # time-ordered; higher = harder
      x: number[]?                 # optional cycle-index / timestamp clock
    LocalDifficultyState: enum [ warming_up, steady, struggling, easing ]
    LocalDifficulty:
      unitId: string
      unitKind: UnitKind?
      curvature: CurvaturePoint|null   # the B1 read at the latest sample; null while warming up
      accelerationZ: number|null       # acceleration in units of this unit's own noise
      flagged: boolean                 # |z| crossed the alarm threshold
      state: LocalDifficultyState
      samples: number

  api:
    assessLocalDifficulty(series, options?) -> LocalDifficulty:
      >  Run curvature over one unit's series and classify the most recent point.
         options are curvature/alarm options (window, minSamples, threshold, noiseWindow).
    rankLocalDifficulty(seriesList, options?) -> LocalDifficulty[]:
      >  Assess each unit; return most-struggling-first (struggling > easing > steady >
         warming_up; ties broken by larger own-noise excursion). Severity order only —
         NOT a drill/defer decision (criticality lives in C1). Returns a sorted copy;
         does not reorder the caller's array.

  classification:
    - warming_up:  curvature is null (below the min-cycle gate)
    - struggling:  flagged AND acceleration > 0
    - easing:      flagged AND acceleration < 0
    - steady:      not flagged

  guarantees:
    - steady_never_flags: an even-paced (sloped) unit reads steady (baseline-free curvature)
    - boundary_agnostic: boundaries are ordinary units with unitKind='boundary'; no special logic
    - pure: no mutation of caller input; no app/storage coupling

  tests: packages/core/src/learning/localDifficulty.test.ts   # 9 cases
